  <content>
  <section><para>Welcome to your Algebra I resource page! Here, you'll discover essential tools to support your journey through teaching this curriculum. </para>

<div class="os-raise-accordion">
    <!-- Accordion Item 1-->
    <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">Course Narrative Overview</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para>Students begin the year by exploring linear equations, inequalities, and systems of linear equations and inequalities (Units 1 - 2). This builds on the content they learned in middle school mathematics. They use representations to model relationships and constraints but also reason with them abstractly. Students write, rearrange, evaluate, and solve equations and inequalities, explaining and validating their reasoning with increased precision. They then take these insights to a unit on two-variable statistics (Unit 3), where they extend their prior knowledge of scatter plots to determine lines of best fit. Students use correlation coefficients to assess linear models, interpret quantitative data, and distinguish correlation and causality. </para>
            <para>Next, students study functions and then deepen their ability to represent, interpret, and communicate about them—using function notation, domain and range, average rate of change, and features of graphs (Unit 4). This exploration of the traits of linear functions is followed by exponential (Unit 5) and then quadratic functions in later units (Units 7 - 9). For each function type, students begin their investigation with real-world and mathematical contexts, look closely at the structural attributes of the function, and analyze how these attributes are expressed in different representations.
            </para>
            <para>Before investigating quadratic functions, however, students expand their understanding of applying operations to polynomial expressions (Unit 6). Students begin by reviewing their prior knowledge for how to simplify expressions containing exponents before extending the knowledge to include negative and rational exponents. Then, they engage in applying these skills within the mathematical context of multiplying and dividing polynomials. 
            </para>
            <para>The course ends with a close look at quadratic equations. Students extend their ability to use equations to model relationships and solve problems. They develop their capacity to write, transform, graph, and solve equations—by reasoning, rearranging equations into useful forms, and applying the quadratic formula. In solving quadratic equations, students encounter rational and irrational solutions; providing an opportunity to deepen their understanding of the real number system.
            </para>
            <para>In summary, students progress from linear to exponential to quadratic functions by exploring what makes the functions different. Students are asked to consider what happens when a pattern is generated from a specific value that is repeatedly added or subtracted (linear). Then, they engage in thinking about patterns that are generated from a specific value that is repeatedly multiplied or divided (exponential). Embedded in this process, students explore arithmetic and geometric sequences. Then, in later units, students are asked to consider patterns that adhere to a model that both increases and decreases over time (quadratic). Throughout each phase of learning, students encounter tables, equations, graphs, and verbal descriptions of real world and mathematical scenarios. 
            </para>
        </div>
    </div>
    <!-- Accordion Item 2-->
    <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">Scope and Sequence</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para>The following suggested scope and sequence is based on a 45-minute class period. The total number of school days to complete this scope and sequence is approximately 180 days. 
            </para>
            <para>Adjustments can be made in order to personalize the slope and sequence to the specific needs of students. Alternate pacing suggestions are also available in the “Getting Started” section of the course. </para>
            <para>All of the “Mathematical Process Standards'' (A.1A, A.1B, A.1C, A.1D, A.1E, A.1F, A.1G) are embedded throughout every unit. Note that process standards which are significant to the learning processes for a specific lesson have been highlighted on the list of TEKS in the teacher guide for those lessons</para>
        
            <table class="os-raise-textheavyadjustedtable">
                <thead>
                  <row>
                    <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Unit</entry>
                    <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Learning Goals</entry>
                    <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Content TEKS</entry>
                  </row>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>1: Linear Equations
                        (Approximately 21 days)</para>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>
                        Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Write and solve linear equations and inequalities given multiple representations of relations.</item>
                            <item>Determine the slope of a line given multiple representations of linear equations.
                            </item>
                            <item>Represent linear equations in various forms (standard, slope-intercept, point slope).
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>
                        <para>A.2B, A.2C, A.2D, A.2E, A.2F, A.2G, A.3A, A.3C, A.5A, A.12E</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>2: Linear Inequalities and Systems
                        (Approximately 22 days)
                        </para></entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Write systems of two linear equations and solve the systems by graphing, substitution, and elimination.
                            </item>
                            <item>Solve linear inequalities in one and two variables.
                            </item>
                            <item>Graph the solution set of linear inequalities in two variables and systems of two linear inequalities in two variables on the coordinate plane.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry><entry><para>A.2H, A.2I, A.3D, A.3F, A.3G, A.3H, A.5B, A.5C</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>3: Two-Variable Statistics
                        (Approximately 12 days)</para>
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Write, with and without technology, linear functions that provide a reasonable fit to data to estimate solutions and make predictions for real-world problems.
                            </item>
                            <item>Calculate, using technology, the correlation coefficient between two quantitative variables and interpret this quantity as a measure of the strength of the linear association.
                            </item>
                            <item>Compare and contrast association and causation in real-world problems.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry><entry>A.4A, A.4B, A.4C
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>4: Functions
                        (Approximately 27 days)</para>
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Decide whether relations define a function and evaluate functions expressed in function notation.
                            </item>
                            <item>Graph linear functions on the coordinate plane and identify key features, identify transformations of the parent function 
                            </item>
                            <item>\(𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥\), and represent domain and range using inequalities for real-world situations.
                            </item>
                            <item>Identify terms of arithmetic and geometric sequences and write a formula for the nth term of arithmetic and geometric sequences, given the value of several of their terms.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.2A, A.2B, A.2C, A.3A, A.3B, A.3C, A.3E, A.7A, A.12A, A.12B, A.12C, A.12D</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para/>5: Introduction to Exponential Functions
                        (Approximately 21 days)
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Simplify numeric and algebraic expressions using the laws of exponents and simplify numerical radical expressions involving square roots.
                            </item>
                            <item>Interpret the meaning of the values of 𝑎 and 𝑏 in exponential functions of the form \(𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑎𝑏^𝑥\) in real-world problems and write exponential functions to describe problems arising from mathematical and real-world situations, including growth and decay.
                            </item>
                            <item>Graph exponential functions that model growth and decay and identify key features.
                            </item>
                            <item>Compare linear and exponential functions.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.2B, A.2D, A.2E, A.2F, A.3B, A.3E, A.9A, A.9B, A.9C, A.9D, A.9E, A.11B</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>Fall STAAR Review
                        (Approximately 2 days)
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Assess their understanding of content regarding linear and exponential functions. </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.2A, A.2C, A.2I, A.3B, A.3C, A.3D, A.5A, A.5C, A.7A</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>6: Working with Polynomials
                        (Approximately 14 days)</para>
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Add, subtract, multiply, and divide polynomials of degree one and degree two.
                            </item>
                            <item>Rewrite polynomial expressions using the distributive property.
                            </item>
                            <item>Factor trinomials in the form \(𝑎𝑥^2+𝑏𝑥+𝑐\), including perfect square trinomials of degree two, and factor binomials that are the difference of two squares.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.10A, A.10B, A.10C, A.10D, A.10E, A.10F, A.12B</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>7: Introduction to Quadratic Functions
                        (Approximately 24 days)</para>
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>                            Identify quadratic functions from expressions, tables, and graphs.
                            </item>
                            <item>Represent quadratic equations in standard and factored form.
                            </item>
                            <item>Graph quadratic equations from standard form, factored form, and vertex form.
                            </item>
                            <item>Describe how the 𝑎, 𝑏, and 𝑐 in \(𝑎𝑥^2+𝑏𝑥+𝑐\) affect the graph of a quadratic equation.
                            </item>
                            <item>Determine whether a visual pattern represents a linear, exponential, or quadratic relationship.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.6A, A.6B, A.6C, A.7A, A.7B, A.7C, A.10B, A.12B </para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>8: Quadratic Equations
                        (Approximately 18 days)</para>
                        </entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Solve quadratic equations by factoring and using the zero product property. 
                            </item>
                            <item>Write quadratic functions when given real solutions and graphs of their related equations.
                            </item>
                            <item>Write quadratic functions using technology to find a reasonable fit to data.
                            </item>
                        </list></entry>
                    
                    <entry>
                        <para>A.8A, A.8B</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>9: More Quadratic Equations 
                        (Approximately 17 days) 
                        </para></entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Simplify radical expressions involving square roots. 
                            </item>
                            <item>Solve quadratic equations by taking the square root, completing the square, and using the quadratic formula.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.6B, A.7A, A.8A, A.11A</para> 
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry><para>Spring STAAR Review
                        (Approximately 2 days)
                        </para></entry>
                    <entry>Students will:
                        <list>
                            <item>Assess their understanding of content regarding linear and exponential functions.
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><para>A.3B, A.6A, A.7A, A.7C, A.8A, A.9C, A.9D, A.10E, A.11B</para>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </table>
        </div>
    </div>
    <!-- Accordion Item 3-->
    <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">ELPS Dot Chart</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/55f2625e2988a678f3dee5b3b00a1e1feb8e6f8e">Access this chart</link> to learn about the English Language Proficiency Standards (ELPS) coverage throughout the curriculum.</para>
        </div>
    </div>
    <!-- Accordion Item 4-->
    <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">TEKS Dot Chart</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1808a9dd4ef1850a03ec8d0433a333fe035ad7e9">Access this chart</link> to learn about the Texas Essential Knowledge and Skills coverage throughout the curriculum.</para>
        </div>
    </div>
    <!-- Accordion Item 5-->
    <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">Student Resources</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para>We recommend having your students keep the following resources readily available as they progress through the Algebra I course:</para>
            <list type="enumerated">
                <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fd91c9a8ce0515f1a870ae27ec98e9145b671fae">English-Spanish Glossary</link>
                </item>
                <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c3c8bc2a5c56edc25d081718253537afc465a052">Algebra 1 STAAR Reference Materials</link>
                </item>
                <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d4ee3177218a361103258f3caef85ce6bd387457">Algebra 1 STAAR Graph Paper</link>
                </item>
            </list>
        </div>
    </div>
     <!-- Accordion Item 5-->
     <div class="os-raise-accordion-item">
        <button class="os-raise-accordion-header">End of Course Exam Preparation</button>
        <div class="os-raise-accordion-content">
            <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7f6968c34d6a73ab4d7fcf3bd532baedf1828b21">Access this document</link> to review the content in this course, to prepare for the STAAR end of course exam. Use data to determine which topics are most needed for your students. The document includes supplementary activities for each topic, offering diverse instructional approaches. For every topic, three activities are provided: two independent tasks and one interactive or game-based activity.</para>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<para>If you have any trouble accessing any of the support resources linked here, please email k12@openstax.org and our team will be happy to help.</para>
</section>
</content>
